WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Cloud network security is about controlling what is reachable, from where,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.340
and for what purpose.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.320
A cloud service may be private, internal to a virtual network,

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.620
exposed through a controlled entry point, or reachable from the public internet.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:19.960
Public exposure is not automatically wrong. Many applications need public endpoints.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.140
The security question is whether that exposure is intentional, minimal, protected,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.800
and monitored.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.060
Cloud networks are built from several control layers. Security groups, firewall rules,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.920
routing tables, load balancers, private endpoints, network access controls,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.020
and service-level settings all influence reachability.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.480
A resource can look private at one layer and still be reachable through another path.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.900
That is why network review should focus on effective exposure,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.960
not just one setting in one place.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:55.460
Segmentation limits unnecessary communication. A public web tier might need to receive

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.600
traffic from users and connect to a private application tier.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:05.100
The application tier might need limited access to a database. The database usually should

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.480
not accept connections from the internet,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.660
from every workload, or from every team network.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.840
Segmentation helps contain mistakes and makes normal communication easier to reason about.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:21.020
Good firewall and security group rules are specific. They avoid broad source ranges unless

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.080
there is a documented need.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.860
They limit ports to what the service actually uses. They name ownership and purpose

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:28.880
clearly.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.220
They are reviewed when applications change. Temporary access rules should expire or be

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.040
removed after use.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.580
A rule that was safe during a migration can become risky if it is forgotten for months.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.320
Network controls also connect to identity and data protection. A private endpoint does not

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:47.740
replace IAM.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:53.000
A restrictive firewall rule does not replace encryption. A public endpoint does not have

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.900
to mean weak access control.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.400
These controls work together. The safest design usually combines intentional exposure,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:06.380
strong identity, encrypted transport, service-level authorization, logging, and alerting.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.240
A practical network review asks four questions. What is reachable from the internet?

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.860
What can talk to what inside the cloud environment? Which ports and protocols are open?

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.160
Which services should be private or reachable only through controlled entry points?

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.940
Teams should document the expected answers and compare them against actual configuration.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.520
When the actual network does not match the intended architecture,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:34.260
the next step is safe remediation: tighten rules, add segmentation, update owners,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.320
and monitor for recurrence.
